       "id": "statistics_probability-survivalanalysiscompetingrisks-02",
       "title": "Robust Survival Analysis Competing Risks under Distribution Shift",
       "authors": [
        "Anonymous"
       ],
       "year": 2025,
       "venue": "illustrative",
       "keywords": [
        "Survival Analysis Competing Risks",
        "Robust"
       ],
       "angle": "Robust",
       "verified": true,
       "abstract": "This paper studies **Survival Analysis Competing Risks**, grounded in a real, citable literature, and proposes a focused method with illustrative experiments.",
       "sections": [
        {
         "title": "1. Introduction",
         "content": "Survival Analysis Competing Risks is an active research area. We target a concrete gap identified from recent literature."
        },
        {
         "title": "2. Related Work (real literature)",
         "content": "- Kanda (2012) — \"Investigation of the freely available easy-to-use software ‘EZR’ for medical statistics\", Bone Marrow Transplantation (cited 18981).\n- Fine et al. (1999) — \"A Proportional Hazards Model for the Subdistribution of a Competing Risk\", Journal of the American Statistical Association (cited 13695).\n- Chalasani et al. (2017) — \"The diagnosis and management of nonalcoholic fatty liver disease: Practice guidance from the American Association for the Study of Liver Diseases\", Hepatology (cited 7338).\n- Ray (1988) — \"A Class of $K$-Sample Tests for Comparing the Cumulative Incidence of a Competing Risk\", The Annals of Statistics (cited 4992).\n- Chalasani et al. (2012) — \"The diagnosis and management of non-alcoholic fatty liver disease: Practice Guideline by the American Association for the Study of Liver Diseases, American College of Gastroenterology, and the American Gastroenterological Association\", Hepatology (cited 3828).\n- Hosmer et al. (1999) — \"Applied survival analysis regression modeling of time to event data\" (cited 3040)."
        },
        {
         "title": "3. Method",
         "content": "We model distribution shift explicitly and optimise a worst-case objective over an uncertainty set of plausible test distributions. We instantiate this idea for Survival Analysis Competing Risks."
        },
        {
         "title": "4. Experiments (Illustrative)",
         "content": "| System | Primary Metric | Efficiency |\n|---|---|---|\n| Strong baseline | 0.812 | 1.00× |\n| Ablation | 0.828 | 0.92× |\n| **Ours** | **0.851** | **0.71×** |\n\n*Metrics simulated to illustrate intended behaviour; not measured.*"
        },
        {
         "title": "5. Conclusion",
         "content": "We connected a real literature to a concrete method for Survival Analysis Competing Risks."
        }
       ],
